<section class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Meet the founder</span><h1>Engineering a bridge for the next generation.</h1><p class="lede">A life shaped in Mill Creek, Arkansas. A career built through education and technology. A commitment carried forward to Accra.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap grid-2"><div class="founder-placeholder" role="img" aria-label="Founder portrait placeholder"><div><span>Founder portrait needed</span><small>Replace with an approved high-resolution portrait before launch.</small></div></div><div><div class="draft-note"><strong>Editorial placeholder:</strong> Add the founder’s verified name, engineering field, education, and professional background before publication.</div><h2 style="margin-top:28px">Making a way—and leaving the way wider.</h2><p>I grew up in Mill Creek, Arkansas, where resources could be limited but the strength of community was not. Education and technology gave me a path to become an engineer. Just as important were the people who saw possibility in me and encouraged me to keep going.</p><p>That journey taught me that talent does not belong to one place, one income level, or one set of circumstances. What is unevenly distributed is access: access to tools, instruction, networks, and the chance to practice until confidence takes root.</p><p>Mill Creek-AR Learning Center is a way of reaching back and reaching across. It connects the perseverance I learned in Arkansas with the creativity and ambition I see in young people in Accra.</p><div class="pull-quote">Success means more when it becomes a bridge someone else can cross.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="section cream"><div class="wrap grid-2"><div><span class="eyebrow">Lifting as we climb</span><h2>A tradition of education, self-determination, and community.</h2></div><div><p class="lede">Across the African diaspora, generations have used knowledge, mutual support, and persistence to expand what is possible. This project honors that tradition without turning struggle into spectacle.</p><p>The goal is not to prescribe a future for students. It is to equip them to shape their own—with practical skills, mentorship, and a community that takes their ambitions seriously.</p><a class="button button-outline" href="adopt.php">Help widen the bridge</a></div></div></section>
